.TH MEMFS 4
.SH NAME
memfs \- memory file system
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B memfs
[
.I srvname
]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Memfs
serves a directory tree of any shape, kept entirely on the heap: files that
grow as they are written, made and removed by name, listed, and truncated.
It is what a shell needs from a filesystem before a disk is mounted.
.PP
.I Memfs
posts
.B /srv/memfs
.RI ( /srv/ srvname
when given), forks, and the parent exits once the name is there, so a shell
that started it can mount on the next line:
.IP
.EX
memfs
mount /srv/memfs /mnt
.EE
.PP
A file is made with an
.IR open (5)
create and removed by naming it in a
.BR Tremove ;
unlike the fixed-tree servers in this section,
.B Tremove
here removes the file named and does not stop the server.
A directory must be empty to be removed.
Permission bits are kept per file; there is no owner and no group, so every
file is everyone's.
.PP
The server ends when its posted name and every mount of it are gone, which
the kernel turns into a hang-up on the pipe, or on a note it does not
handle.
.SH FILES
.TP
.BI /srv/ srvname
the posted service,
.B memfs
by default
.SH SOURCE
.B servers/memfs
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR bind (1),
.IR serve (2),
.IR fork (2),
.IR open (5),
.IR clunk (5)
.SH BUGS
There is no rename.
.I Cp
and
.I mv
(see
.IR cp (1))
copy and remove instead.
